docs/decisions.md:1559:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1563:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
docs/decisions.md:1571:| OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
docs/decisions.md:1613:| OD151 | <a id="od151"></a>Module paths of more than one level (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether a qualified path may name a member of a module nested more than one level deep (`a::b::f()`). Bears on SYN-10, MOD-4 and OPEN-139. The current compiler: the parser's lowercase module-path branch accepts one qualification pair: `a::b::f()`, with `f` in `mod b` inside `mod a`, is a parse error on both consumers (E0001, `expected <, got ident(f)`), because after `a::b` it accepts only `::<` (explicit type arguments). SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 gives `std::fs::read_to_string` as an example of module qualification, which this parser does not accept. Options: (a) one qualification pair, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 says so; (b) any depth, every segment before the last naming a module (a repetition, which stays LL(1)). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-151 | see OPEN-151 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): multi-level paths are written in import declarations; an imported item is used qualified by its module's last segment (`b::f` for `a::b::f`).** |
